  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Fri May 29 2026\n\nBeneath the leeward side of an upper level omega block, high \npressure will dominate the Great Lakes region today leading to a\npleasant and warm end to the work week. As the surface high, \ncentered over lower Michigan early this morning, continues to \npull away from the local area, winds will turn southerly after \ndaybreak helping temperatures to climb into the middle 80s this \nafternoon away from the lake. A lake breeze is expected to \nprotrude inland beginning late this morning keeping communities \nalong the lakeshore likely stuck in the upper 60s to lower 70s \ntoday with the coolest conditions anticipated on the IL side of \nthe lake. Some marginally breezy winds may be found behind the \nlake breeze as it progresses inland, otherwise expect generally \nlight winds today with sunshine filtered by some high level \ncloud cover.\n\nThis upper blocking pattern looks to hold for several days \nwhich will keep relative high pressure parked over the Great \nLakes well into next week. This will result in a very quiet and \ndry stretch of weather through the next week. A second center of\nhigh pressure will drop into the region from the north early \ntomorrow bringing slightly cooler, some may say more \ncomfortable, conditions to the weekend. Highs are progged in the\n70s and lower 80s both Saturday and Sunday with notably cooler \nconditions forecast toward the lakeshore both days, again with \nthe coolest conditions expected on the IL side of the lake. So \nwhile it will feel like great beach-going weather inland this \nweekend, expect noticeably cooler conditions on Lake Michigan \nbeaches.\n\nAnother word of caution for weekend beachgoers: Breezy onshore \nwinds will lead to a High Swim Risk with dangerous waves and \ncurrents on Saturday at Illinois Lake Michigan beaches and \ngenerally unfavorable swimming conditions at Indiana beaches.\nDue to the anticipated High Swim Risk, a Beach Hazards Statement\nwill be issued for Saturday and Saturday night for Illinois \nLake Michigan beaches. Less than favorable swimming conditions \nmay linger into early Sunday as well, particularly in Illinois.\n\nRegular chances for rain are forecast into next week west of \nthe Mississippi along a stagnant baroclinic zone stirring on the\nwestern fringe of the surface high. Sunday night into Monday, \nsome medium-range guidance suggests some of this baroclinicity \ncould meander east and bring scattered rain chances to parts of \nwestern and central IL, but dry conditions are favored around \nour CWA. Couldn't argue with the NBM's slight chance PoPs in our\nsouthwest CWA Sunday night. Those will be our only mentionable \nPoPs in the forecast for the next week as the blocking pattern \nis progged to break and reinforce high pressure around the \nregion during the middle part of next week. Support for precip \nbuilds toward the end of next week as the high finally looks to \neject out over the Atlantic.\n\nDoom",
